From ebf42c79b0aeb6f46f3e90553eb18f16b6c7e298 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bench <bench@local>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:25:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 59/65] perf(msm): contiguous per-step IFMA drain buffer, 512
 pairs per drain

The IFMA batch-affine drain kept its 26 limb columns as separate CAP-wide
rows, so the columns sat a multiple of 2 KiB apart and every vector step
touched 26 lines in the same few L1 sets. Each 8-pair step's columns now
live in one contiguous 1664-byte group, and with the conflict misses gone a
drain can hold 512 pairs, halving the number of field inversions per pair
in the Stage 6a tree reduce and the Stage 6b batched adds.

Output unchanged: the drain computes the same affine sums of the same
pairs (one Montgomery batch per drain, exact field arithmetic); only where
the queued operands live and how many pairs share an inversion change, and
affine point addition has a unique result. MSM results, and so every
commitment, are identical.

The IFMA edge-case test fills the buffer through push() instead of writing the
column arrays directly, and calls drain_to() (the pointer-output overload); it
did not compile on the starting series.
